Figure 10 illustrates immunofluorescence micrographs of skeletal muscle injection sites 24 hours post-injection, demonstrating Nano-11's ability to induce local inflammation and facilitate antigen uptake. The top row shows significant infiltration of Mac-2+ monocytes/macrophages and MHCII+ cells, which contain intracellular AF647-labeled Nano-11 (red), along with Ly-6G+ neutrophils (green), when Nano-11 is co-injected with OVA. In contrast, the bottom row for soluble OVA alone shows minimal inflammatory cell recruitment. This highlights that Nano-11 strongly enhances the delivery of antigens to, and uptake by, specific immune cells at the injection site.